  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186069948,
      "id": "b1ed2594-7cf6-57f4-bc81-3b89ff11faa4",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "The **quadratic formula** is a formula that we can use to compute the solution of a quadratic equation.\n\nGiven a quadratic equation in standard form\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe formula for the solutions to this equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic formula is derived using a method called *completing the square*. You'll learn more about this method and how it's used to derive the quadratic formula in future lessons.\n\nTo see how applying the quadratic formula works in practice, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\nx^2-2x-15=0\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of this quadratic equation are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a=1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}b=-2}, \\qquad {\\color{purple}c=-15}\n$$\n\nTo compute the solution, we substitute the above values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(-15)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{64}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 8}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 4 = 5 \\\\\nx &= 1 - 4 =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our quadratic equation are $x=-3$ and $x=5$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "1629",
      "stepId": "17592186069817",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068637,
      "id": "0e329ac2-97a6-53b1-95fe-6baa18d4a70a",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068793,
        "title": "Applying the Quadratic Formula Using a Framework"
      },
      "markdown": "Solving the equation $25x^{2}-20x+4= 0$ using the quadratic formula leads to the expression below:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{20 \\pm \\sqrt{(-20)^2-4\\cdot  25 \\cdot  \\boxed{ {\\phantom{00}}}}} {2 \\cdot 25}\n$$\nWhat number is missing from the formula, and what is the solution of the equation?",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-7054",
      "stepId": "17592186069715",
      "title": "Applying the Quadratic Formula Using a Framework",
      "workedSolution": "The coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na=25, \\quad b=-20, \\quad c=4\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-20) ± \\sqrt{(-20)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot (4)})/(2 \\cdot 25) \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{20^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot \\color{blue}\\,4\\,}}{2 \\cdot 25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\boxed{ { {\\color{blue}\\hspace{0.167em} 4 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}.$\n\nNow, to find the solution, we simplify the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{20^{2} - 4 \\cdot 25 \\cdot \\color{blue}\\,4\\,}}{2 \\cdot 25} &= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{400 - 400}}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± \\sqrt{0}}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 ± 0}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068500,
      "id": "2361dbb5-1b9b-5ce9-95ec-f0f055d1a594",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068862,
        "title": "Using the Quadratic Formula to Solve a Quadratic Equation"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve for $x$ where $2x^2 - 3x - 5 = 0$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1434",
      "stepId": "17592186069818",
      "title": "Using the Quadratic Formula to Solve a Quadratic Equation",
      "workedSolution": "The coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 2, \\quad b = -3, \\quad c = -5\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(-3) \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4(2)(-5)}}{2(2)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{49}}{4} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{3 \\pm 7}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{3 + 7}{4} = \\frac{10}{4} = \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 - 7}{4} =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068501,
      "id": "2f4ba00b-e0cb-527e-b317-77580216be52",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068863,
        "title": "Simplifying and Solving a Quadratic Equation"
      },
      "markdown": "Solve the equation $x^2 + 2x = 6$.",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1435",
      "stepId": "17592186069819",
      "title": "Simplifying and Solving a Quadratic Equation",
      "workedSolution": "The first step is to put the equation into standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x &= 6 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x - 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\quad b = 2, \\quad c = -6\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(2) \\pm \\sqrt{(2)^2 - 4(1)(-6)}}{2(1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{28}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= -1 \\pm \\sqrt{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = -1 + \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx = -1 - \\sqrt{7}\n$$"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186068502,
      "id": "9bb1a622-6413-5d78-9841-3800eebb1a9d",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186068864,
        "title": "Applying the Quadratic Formula To an Equation With No Real Solutions"
      },
      "markdown": "What are the solutions to $3x^2 = x - 10$?",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-1436",
      "stepId": "17592186069820",
      "title": "Applying the Quadratic Formula To an Equation With No Real Solutions",
      "workedSolution": "The first step is to put the equation into standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2} - x + 10 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients are\n\n$$\na = 3, \\quad b = -1, \\quad c = 10\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-1) ± \\sqrt{(-1)^{2} - 4(3)(10)})/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 ± \\sqrt{1 - 120}}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 ± \\sqrt{-119}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that the equation has no real solutions because $\\sqrt{-119}$ is not a real number."
    }
  ],
